│ patch_description        │ The current search has two weaknesses. First, it only accepts strictly-improving moves    │
│                          │ and returns the *last* configuration, so a bad accepted move can never be undone — we     │
│                          │ must track the global best configuration separately (current version even has a bug:      │
│                          │ `best_points` is compared after the loop but `points` may have drifted below it, and the  │
│                          │ final comparison logic returns the worse of the two). Second, the warm start uses a       │
│                          │ coarse triangular lattice that leaves points clustered, giving a poor basin. I propose:   │
│                          │ (1) an improved warm start using a refined symmetric construction spread across the whole │
│                          │ triangle, (2) correct global-best tracking during the search, (3) a two-phase schedule:   │
│                          │ coarse exploration with occasional simultaneous two-point moves (which escape one-point   │
│                          │ local optima typical in Heilbronn arrangements), then fine refinement, and (4) bounded    │
│                          │ restarts from perturbed copies of the best solution if the search stalls. All moves are   │
│                          │ still clipped into the triangle and evaluated with the same fast vectorized triplet       │
│                          │ computation, keeping determinism via the fixed seed.                                      │
