│ patch_name               │ many_restarts_time_budget                                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ The current program scores 0.00, which strongly suggests the optimizer gets stuck in      │
│                          │ degenerate (collinear) configurations — the lattice warm starts contain many collinear    │
│                          │ triplets with zero area, and the simulated-annealing acceptance rule (`val >= cur -       │
│                          │ T*1e-3*rng.random() or val > cur`) is non-standard and too permissive, letting the walk   │
│                          │ drift while the "best" snapshot may still be near-degenerate. Two targeted fixes inspired │
│                          │ by standard best practice for the Heilbronn problem: (1) repair degenerate warm starts by │
│                          │ adding jitter so every restart begins with a positive minimum area, and (2) replace the   │
│                          │ SA with a strict improving-move local search (move a vertex of the currently-worst        │
│                          │ triangle, accept only strict improvements with margin), wrapped in a                      │
│                          │ best-of-many-restarts loop with an anytime time budget (~25s). Since the prior successful │
│                          │ pattern (per the recommendation) was "more restarts of a proven search," and search       │
│                          │ quality varies across random starts, best-of-36 is the lowest-risk path to a higher score │
│                          │ while keeping the deterministic seed for reproducibility.                                 │
