│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ kworst_triplet_guided_polish                                                              │
│ patch_description        │ The current random single-point polish is undirected: it picks a random point and hopes   │
│                          │ it helps. Following the recommendation, we should identify the bottom-k worst triplets    │
│                          │ and concentrate the search effort on the points participating in them, evaluating many    │
│                          │ candidate displacements vectorized at once. Concretely: (1) compute all triplet areas,    │
│                          │ (2) take the worst triplet's three vertices as the "hot" point set (plus occasionally the │
│                          │ bottom-10 triplets' union for wider moves), (3) generate a batch of candidate             │
│                          │ perturbations for each hot point, evaluate all of them with one vectorized area           │
│                          │ computation, and accept the best improving candidate. This gives the search many escape   │
│                          │ directions from local minima while reusing the existing vectorized `_areas` machinery,    │
│                          │ and it evaluates dozens of moves for the cost of roughly one. We also raise the polish    │
│                          │ budget slightly since batched evaluation is cheap, and keep a global-best tracker to      │
│                          │ never lose an improvement.                                                                │
