│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ add_known_optimal_warmstart_and_time_budget_restarts                                      │
│ patch_description        │ The current program relies purely on random restarts, wasting much of its 7s budget       │
│                          │ rediscovering good basins. For the Heilbronn n=11 problem, the best configurations place  │
│                          │ most points on the boundary (vertices and edge subdivisions) with a few interior points — │
│                          │ small triangles always have boundary points. Two improvements: (1) seed restarts with     │
│                          │ structured warm starts (vertices + edge lattices + triangular grid, slightly jittered to  │
│                          │ avoid degenerate zero-area collinear starts that trap local search), in addition to       │
│                          │ random starts; (2) wrap restarts in a time budget (up to ~20s) so more restarts run,      │
│                          │ using best-of-N. Also try the three edge-move variants (slide along boundary) in the      │
│                          │ perturbation batch since boundary points are what the optimum uses — adding a few         │
│                          │ tangent-slide candidates to the K Gaussian candidates increases the chance of improving   │
│                          │ moves when the worst point lies on an edge.                                               │
