│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ directed_worst_triplet_escape                                                             │
│ patch_description        │ The prior programs failed because SLSQP soft-min optimization got stuck (score 0.22) or   │
│                          │ crashed (score 0). Inspired by the barycentric-restart and worst-triplet-directed ideas   │
│                          │ in the context programs, I implement a fundamentally different approach: a pure numpy     │
│                          │ "worst-triplet escape" local search. Each iteration identifies the globally worst         │
│                          │ triangle, then attempts to move one of its three vertices *directly away from the line    │
│                          │ through the other two vertices* (the exact gradient direction of that triangle's area),   │
│                          │ at multiple step scales, plus random perturbations and occasional full-point relocations. │
│                          │ This is wrapped in deterministic seeded restarts with a global-best tracker, and —        │
│                          │ following the recommendation — a final merge phase that combines the points least         │
│                          │ involved in small triangles from the two best restart configurations, followed by a       │
│                          │ polish pass. Everything is vectorized over all 165 triplets, robust to failures, with a   │
│                          │ guaranteed-valid barycentric fallback.                                                    │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
