│ patch_name               │ gradient_escape_multistart_polish                                                         │
│ patch_description        │ The prior programs that scored 0 all suffered from fragile code paths (placeholder        │
│                          │ points, exception fallbacks yielding degenerate lattices with collinear triplets). The    │
│                          │ best performer (0.31) used gradient-informed "perpendicular escape" moves on the worst    │
│                          │ triplet. I build on that insight but make it robust and stronger: (1) multiple            │
│                          │ deterministic symmetric seed layouts polished in parallel with the best kept; (2)         │
│                          │ vectorized min-area computation over all 165 triplets; (3) for the worst triplet, move    │
│                          │ its points along the exact analytic direction perpendicular to the opposite edge (which   │
│                          │ provably increases that triplet's area), trying all-subset moves with adaptive step; (4)  │
│                          │ periodic random-restart perturbation escape from local optima; (5) strict validity guard  │
│                          │ — the output always has 11 finite, distinct, in-triangle points, with a safe hand-built   │
│                          │ fallback that itself has decent min-area. This combines the multistart robustness idea    │
│                          │ with the directed-move idea while eliminating the failure modes that zeroed out earlier   │
│                          │ scores.                                                                                   │
