╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ directed_perp_moves                                                                       │
│ patch_description        │ Add gradient-informed (directed) perturbation moves to the batched worst-triplet polish   │
│                          │ loop. For each hot point that belongs to a currently-bad triplet, compute the analytic    │
│                          │ escape direction: the unit vector perpendicular to the line through the other two         │
│                          │ vertices of that point's worst triplet, pointing away from the line (which provably       │
│                          │ increases that triplet's area at first order). These directed candidates are appended to  │
│                          │ the existing isotropic Gaussian batch, so half the batch exploits free analytic gradient  │
│                          │ information while the other half keeps exploration diversity. Acceptance is still gated   │
│                          │ on global min-area improvement via `_min_area_batch`, so feasibility and monotonic        │
│                          │ progress are preserved. This replaces wasted random attempts with moves that are locally  │
│                          │ guaranteed to relieve the binding constraint, which should raise the final min-area       │
│                          │ substantially (currently only 0.46 of benchmark).                                         │
