│ patch_description        │ Key insight: the previous programs spent most of their time recomputing ALL 165 triangle  │
│                          │ areas for every candidate move. Since only one point moves per step, only the 45          │
│                          │ triangles containing that point change — so we maintain a full area vector and update     │
│                          │ just the affected entries, making each local-search evaluation ~4x cheaper. This lets us  │
│                          │ afford many more restarts AND a new post-restart crossover/merge phase: we take the top-3 │
│                          │ configurations found by independent restarts, build merged candidates (5 points from one  │
│                          │ config, 6 from another, projected into the triangle), and re-run the shrinking            │
│                          │ directed-perturbation loop on each — exploiting the evidence that independent restarts    │
│                          │ saturate around 0.77 while complementary sub-arrangements may combine better. Moves are   │
│                          │ *directed*: we always perturb a vertex of the current worst triangle (best-of-K batched   │
│                          │ Gaussian candidates), which focuses search effort exactly where the objective is          │
│                          │ bottlenecked. Warm starts are boundary/lattice-heavy (vertices + edge points + jittered   │
│                          │ lattices) since Heilbronn optima are known to place most points on the boundary.          │
