│ patch_name               │ fast_local_search_seeding                                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ The current program relies entirely on Powell over 33 softmax parameters with three       │
│                          │ annealing temperatures per start (6 starts → 18 Powell runs), which is slow (24.5s) and   │
│                          │ gets stuck because softmax-smoothed softmin gradients are weak. A more effective approach │
│                          │ for the Heilbronn problem is a fast direct-coordinate "worst-triangle" local search: at   │
│                          │ each step, identify the minimal-area triangle and push its three vertices away from their │
│                          │ opposite sides (the exact gradient of that triangle's area), projecting back via          │
│                          │ barycentric clipping to maintain feasibility. This exploits the geometry directly (the    │
│                          │ optimal configuration is characterized by many near-equal minimal triangles) and is very  │
│                          │ fast. I add this as a cheap pre-optimization stage that produces a high-quality start;    │
│                          │ Powell then polishes only the single best candidate with a reduced temperature schedule,  │
│                          │ cutting runtime substantially while improving the final min area. The strictly-positive   │
│                          │ guard on the returned configuration is preserved.                                         │
