│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ scipy_softmax_topk_softmin                                                                │
│ patch_description        │ The best-performing prior program (combined_score 0.76) used a scipy Powell optimization  │
│                          │ over softmax-barycentric logits with a smooth softmin objective — the current             │
│                          │ stochastic-climb version regressed to 0.51. The key weakness of that softmin approach is  │
│                          │ gradient dilution: with 165 triples, most are far above the minimum and contribute        │
│                          │ negligible but noisy signal, so Powell wastes iterations. I restore the scipy/softmax     │
│                          │ framework and, per the recommendation, replace the uniform softmin over all 165 triples   │
│                          │ with a top-k (k=20) temperature-annealed log-sum-exp over only the smallest areas, using  │
│                          │ the numerically robust `a.min()` shift. Because only the ~20 binding constraints matter,  │
│                          │ the objective surface is far better conditioned for Powell, and each restart converges    │
│                          │ faster and to better minima. I keep two annealing temperatures and 4 deterministic starts │
│                          │ to stay within runtime budget, and retain the existing `_polish` as a cheap final         │
│                          │ feasibility-aware touch-up that can only help (results are kept only if they improve the  │
│                          │ true min area).                                                                           │
