╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ finer_annealing_plus_argmin_polish                                                        │
│ patch_description        │ The current annealing ladder stops at temp = 1e-3, which is still ~2e-3 of the triangle   │
│                          │ area — far too coarse for the softmin to sharpen onto the true minimum-area triplet. Near │
│                          │ the optimum, dozens of triplets have nearly equal areas, so optimizing a blurry softmin   │
│                          │ wastes effort balancing non-binding constraints while the binding one stays               │
│                          │ under-improved. Following the recommendation, I extend the annealing ladder with much     │
│                          │ finer absolute temperatures (1e-4, 1e-5, 1e-6), keeping the a.min()-shifted,              │
│                          │ overflow-safe formulation. After the fine stages, I add an exact-argmin polish: identify  │
│                          │ the truly worst triplet and perturb only its three points via softmax-reparameterized     │
│                          │ Powell on a reduced problem (only those three points' logits), then keep the result only  │
│                          │ if it strictly improves the exact minimum area. This sharpens the final configuration     │
│                          │ without risking the guarded feasibility machinery.                                        │
